/* book Worked example 2: Q: A 50 Ω coaxial stub filled with dielectric has vp = 2×10⁸ m/s and is 3 cm long. At 1 GHz find its input impedance when the far end is (a) shorted and (b) open, and the inductance or capacitance each one replaces.
   1. λ = vp/f = 2×10⁸/10⁹ = 0.2 m = 20 cm, so d/λ = 3/20 = 0.15 and βd = 2π × 0.15 = 0.942 rad = 54°.
   2. tan βd = tan 54° = 1.376; cot βd = 1/1.376 = 0.727.
   3. (a) Short: Zin = jZ0 tan βd = j50 × 1.376 = j68.8 Ω. Positive reactance, so it is inductive: L = X/ω = 68.8/(2π × 10⁹) = 10.95 nH.
   4. (b) Open: Zin = −jZ0 cot βd = −j50 × 0.727 = −j36.3 Ω. Negative reactance, so it is capacitive: C = 1/(ω|X|) = 1/(2π × 10⁹ × 36.3) = 4.38 pF.
   5. Check: the two stubs have the same length, so Zsc × Zoc = (j68.8)(−j36.3) = 2500 = Z0², as the two formulas require.
   Answer: Shorted: Zin = j68.8 Ω, an inductor of 10.95 nH. Open: Zin = −j36.3 Ω, a capacitor of 4.38 pF. Both hold only near 1 GHz. */
