/* book: the chapter, paragraph by paragraph (first sentences):
   - Take a line of characteristic impedance Z0 feeding a real load ZL.
   - Set Zin = Z0: Z0′²/ZL = Z0, so Z0′ = √(Z0 ZL).
   - Z0 is the source-line impedance in Ω; ZL is the real load resistance in Ω; Z0′ is the quarter-wave section impedance in Ω; vp is the phase velocity in m/s; f0 is the design frequency in Hz; l is the section length in m.
   - The match is exact at f0, and also at 3f0, 5f0, … for the ideal lossless section, because βl = π/2, 3π/2, 5π/2, … there.
   - A stub is a short piece of line connected in shunt (across the line) or in series.
   - For a shunt stub, work in admittance.
   - A shorted stub of characteristic admittance Y0 has Ys = −jY0 cot βls; an open stub has Ys = jY0 tan βls.
   - For a load ZL = RL + jXL the stub position has a closed form.
   - d = (λ/2π) tan⁻¹ t when t ≥ 0 and d = (λ/2π)(π + tan⁻¹ t) when t < 0; RL, XL and Z0 in Ω.
   - Measure d from the load, and ls from the junction to the open or short end.
*/
