/* book exam traps: Using a quarter-wave transformer directly on a complex load: the reactance must be cancelled first (for example with a stub, or a length of line that makes the impedance real), or use a different matching network. | Measuring the stub distance d from the source instead of from the load. | Using c/f for wavelength in a line: use vp/f, where vp is set by the dielectric. | Confusing stub position d with stub length ls; ls is measured from the junction to the open or short end. | Assuming a single quarter-wave section matches over a wide band, or only at f0: it matches over a limited band around f0 and again at 3f0, 5f0, … . */
